{"name":"Curious Blue","hex":"#3d85b8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"curious","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Tending to ask questions, or to want to explore or investigate; inquisitive;  nosy, prying.","Caused by curiosity."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Containing or pertaining to trivalent curium."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/curious"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Curiosity","blocks":[{"text":"Curiosity can be a quality related to inquisitive thinking, such as exploration, investigation, and learning, evident in humans and other animals. It can also refer to something which can cause curiosity. When it is for an object it may be called a curio, and a collection of curios or curiosities may be stored together as a collection called curiosities."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Kom%C3%A9ta_C-2020_F3_%28NEOWISE%29.jpg/330px-Kom%C3%A9ta_C-2020_F3_%28NEOWISE%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curiosity"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Fotothek_df_roe-neg_0006464_003_Sch%C3%BCler%2C_eine_Zeitung_lesend_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Fotothek_df_roe-neg_0006464_003_Sch%C3%BCler%2C_eine_Zeitung_lesend_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Fotothek_df_roe-neg_0006464_003_Sch%C3%BCler%2C_eine_Zeitung_lesend_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Fotothek_df_roe-neg_0006464_003_Sch%C3%BCler%2C_eine_Zeitung_lesend_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A child looking to see what another is reading","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFotothek_df_roe-neg_0006464_003_Sch%C3%BCler%2C_eine_Zeitung_lesend_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Apple_IIGS_Mystery_House_Adventure_Game_Fan_Fair_Tacoma_2024.jpg/500px-Apple_IIGS_Mystery_House_Adventure_Game_Fan_Fair_Tacoma_2024.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Apple_IIGS_Mystery_House_Adventure_Game_Fan_Fair_Tacoma_2024.jpg/1280px-Apple_IIGS_Mystery_House_Adventure_Game_Fan_Fair_Tacoma_2024.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Apple_IIGS_Mystery_House_Adventure_Game_Fan_Fair_Tacoma_2024.jpg/500px-Apple_IIGS_Mystery_House_Adventure_Game_